\title{jcglasso: Joint Conditional Graphical Lasso with Censored and Missing Values}
\description{
The package implements the joint conditional graphical lasso estimator of
Sottile et al. (2025) for \code{K} sub-populations of a conditional Gaussian
graphical model with censored and/or missing values: for each sub-population,
a sparse precision matrix of the predictors, sparse regression coefficients and
a sparse precision matrix of the responses given the predictors are estimated,
with group lasso penalties that encourage similar structures across the
sub-populations.
}
\details{
The main functions are \code{\link[=datajcggm]{datajcggm()}} (data), \code{\link[=jcglasso]{jcglasso()}} (estimation along a
path of tuning parameters), \code{\link[=BIC.jcglasso]{BIC.jcglasso()}} and \code{\link[=select_jcglasso]{select_jcglasso()}} (model
selection), \code{\link[=jcggm]{jcggm()}} (maximum likelihood refit), \code{\link[=to_graph]{to_graph()}} (graphs) and
\code{\link[=rjcggm]{rjcggm()}} (simulation). The model with \code{K = 1} is the conditional graphical
lasso with censored and missing values of package 'cglasso' (Augugliaro et al.,
2023). Functions with the same names in the two packages (e.g., \code{\link[=getMatrix]{getMatrix()}},
\code{\link[=QFun]{QFun()}}, \code{\link[=to_graph]{to_graph()}}) are S3 generics in 'jcglasso' whose default methods call
the functions of 'cglasso': to use both packages, attach 'jcglasso' after
'cglasso'.
}
